      title: "Ars Poetica in a Train Station",
      image: "",
      imageAlt: "",
      text: `The following original poem uses image, sound, and structure to develop an unresolved emotional conflict.\n\nI kept the lantern burning after dawn,\nnot for light, but for the shape of flame\nthat held its narrow argument with air.\nBeyond the harbor, morning loosened slowly,\neach color arriving as if ashamed.\n\nYou said the world forgives what it repeats,\nbut even the salt remembers pressure:\nwater speaking over it all night,\nthen leaving it brighter and no less stone.\nI touched the wick and blackened my thumb.\n\nSo much for proof. The room stayed ordinary.\nA chair, a bowl, the unsent note.\nStill, something in the silence leaned toward speech,\nand would not let me call it peace.`,
